"#### Section E: Advanced Python Concepts (Q101–125)\n",
"\n",
"| # | Question | Keywords | Answer |\n",
"|---|----------|----------|--------|\n",
"| 101 | Garbage collection? | GC, reference counting | • **Reference counting**: each object tracks refs → freed at 0<br>• **Cyclic GC**: detects circular references<br>• `gc.collect()` forces collection<br>• Most memory freed via reference counting |\n",
"| 102 | Reference counting? | refcount, memory | • Each object has a reference count<br>• `sys.getrefcount(obj)` shows count (+1 for arg)<br>• Freed immediately when count = 0<br>• Cannot detect circular references |\n",
"| 103 | Circular references? | Circular, gc, cycle | • Objects reference each other (A→B→A)<br>• Ref counting can't free these<br>• Cyclic GC periodically detects/collects<br>• Use `weakref` to break cycles |\n",
"| 104 | What is `weakref`? | weakref, gc | • Reference that doesn't prevent GC<br>• `weakref.ref(obj)` → returns None when collected<br>• `WeakValueDictionary`, `WeakKeyDictionary`<br>• Used for caches, breaking circular refs |\n",
"| 105 | Memory management? | Memory, arena, heap | • Python manages private heap<br>• pymalloc for small objects (<512 bytes)<br>• Pools and arenas for efficiency<br>• CPython reuses freed memory internally |\n",
"| 106 | Why avoid `__del__`? | __del__, destructor | • Timing is unpredictable<br>• Exceptions silently ignored<br>• Prevents GC of circular refs<br>• Use `with` / context managers instead<br>• Or `weakref.finalize()` |\n",
"| 107 | Truth value testing rules? | Truthy, falsy | • **Falsy**: `None`, `False`, `0`, `0.0`, `''`, `[]`, `()`, `{}`, `set()`<br>• Everything else is **truthy**<br>• Custom: define `__bool__()` or `__len__()`<br>• `bool(obj)` calls `__bool__` first, then `__len__` |\n",
"| 108 | What is monkey patching? | Monkey patching, runtime | • Dynamically modifying classes/modules at runtime<br>• `SomeClass.method = new_method`<br>• Used in testing (mocking)<br>• Pros: flexible, quick fixes<br>• Cons: hard to debug, fragile |\n",
"| 109 | Duck typing in depth? | Duck typing, Protocol | • Python cares about behavior, not type<br>• Any object with `read()` is 'file-like'<br>• Python 3.8+: `Protocol` for static duck typing<br>• Define expected methods without inheritance |\n",
"| 110 | `copy()` vs assignment? | Copy, assignment, ref | • Assignment: another reference to SAME object<br>• `copy.copy()`: new object, same nested refs (shallow)<br>• `copy.deepcopy()`: recursively copies everything<br>• Immutable → assignment fine; mutable nested → deepcopy |\n",
"| 111 | String methods? | String, split, join | • `split()`, `join()`, `strip()`, `replace()`<br>• `startswith()`, `endswith()`, `find()`, `count()`<br>• `upper()`, `lower()`, `title()`, `isdigit()`<br>• Strings immutable → methods return NEW strings |\n",
"| 112 | `__call__` and callable? | __call__, callable | • Makes instance callable like a function<br>• `class Counter: def __call__(self): self.n+=1`<br>• Check with `callable(obj)`<br>• Used for stateful functions |\n",
"| 113 | `__getattr__` vs `__getattribute__`? | __getattr__, attribute | • `__getattribute__` → called for EVERY access<br>• `__getattr__` → called ONLY when lookup fails<br>• Use `__getattr__` for dynamic attributes<br>• Avoid `__getattribute__` (infinite recursion risk) |\n",
"| 114 | Python descriptors? | Descriptor, __get__ | • Objects with `__get__`, `__set__`, `__delete__`<br>• Powers `@property`, `@classmethod`, `@staticmethod`<br>• Data descriptor (`__set__`) > instance dict<br>• Key to Python's attribute lookup |\n",
"| 115 | `enum` module? | Enum, IntEnum, auto | • `class Color(Enum): RED=1; GREEN=2`<br>• Type-safe, ordered, hashable, singletons<br>• `IntEnum` allows integer math<br>• `auto()` auto-assigns values<br>• Better than magic constants |\n",
"| 116 | Regular expressions (`re`)? | Regex, re, pattern | • `re.search(pattern, string)` → first match<br>• `re.findall()` → all matches<br>• `re.sub(pattern, repl, string)` → replace<br>• `re.compile()` → pre-compile for reuse<br>• Common: `\\d+`, `\\w+`, `[a-z]+`, `^…$` |\n",
"| 117 | `logging` module? | Logging, DEBUG, INFO | • Levels: DEBUG < INFO < WARNING < ERROR < CRITICAL<br>• `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)`<br>• Handlers: file, console, email<br>• Always use logging, not print, in production |\n",
"| 118 | `datetime` module? | datetime, timedelta | • `datetime.now()`, `date.today()`<br>• `timedelta(days=7)` → duration<br>• `strftime('%Y-%m-%d')` → format to string<br>• `strptime('2024-01-01', '%Y-%m-%d')` → parse<br>• Use `timezone.utc` for awareness |\n",
"| 119 | Virtual environments? | venv, isolation | • Isolated Python with own packages<br>• `python -m venv myenv`<br>• `source myenv/bin/activate` (Unix)<br>• `pip freeze > requirements.txt`<br>• Essential for dependency management |\n",
"| 120 | `pip` and `requirements.txt`? | pip, package | • `pip install package`, `pip install -r requirements.txt`<br>• Pin exact versions: `requests==2.31.0`<br>• Always use virtual environments<br>• Modern: `poetry` or `pipenv` |\n",
"| 121 | `__name__ == '__main__'`? | __name__, main | • `__name__` = `'__main__'` when run directly<br>• `__name__` = module name when imported<br>• Guard prevents code running on import<br>• Reusable code above, script logic in guard |\n",
"| 122 | Module vs Package? | Module, package, __init__ | • **Module**: single `.py` file<br>• **Package**: directory with `__init__.py`<br>• Packages organize related modules<br>• `from package import module` |\n",
"| 123 | `*` and `**` in definitions? | Star, positional-only | • `*args` → positional args as tuple<br>• `**kwargs` → keyword args as dict<br>• `def f(a, /, b, *, c):`<br>  → `a` positional-only, `c` keyword-only<br>• Unpack: `f(*list)`, `f(**dict)` |\n",
"| 124 | Method chaining? | Chaining, fluent | • `obj.filter(x).sort().take(5)`<br>• Return `self` from each method<br>• Common in Pandas, SQLAlchemy<br>• Builder pattern |\n",
"| 125 | `functools.wraps`? | wraps, decorator | • Preserves original `__name__`, `__doc__`, `__module__`<br>• Use in every decorator: `@wraps(func)`<br>• Without it, decorated function appears as wrapper |\n",
