"#### Section F: Testing, Modules & Standard Library (Q126–150)\n",
"\n",
"| # | Question | Keywords | Answer |\n",
"|---|----------|----------|--------|\n",
"| 126 | Unit testing? | unittest, pytest | • Verifies individual functions work correctly<br>• `unittest`: class-based, `TestCase`, `assertEqual`<br>• `pytest`: function-based, plain `assert`, fixtures<br>• Run: `pytest test_module.py -v` |\n",
"| 127 | `unittest` vs `pytest`? | unittest, pytest | • `unittest`: class-based, built-in, verbose<br>• `pytest`: function-based, simpler, better output<br>• `pytest` has fixtures, parametrize, plugins<br>• `pytest` is industry standard |\n",
"| 128 | Mocking in tests? | Mock, patch | • `unittest.mock.Mock()` → fake objects<br>• `@patch('module.func')` → replace during tests<br>• `mock.return_value = 42`<br>• `mock.assert_called_with(arg)`<br>• Isolate units from external deps |\n",
"| 129 | TDD? | TDD, red-green-refactor | • **Red**: write failing test<br>• **Green**: write minimal code to pass<br>• **Refactor**: clean up while tests pass<br>• Write tests BEFORE code |\n",
"| 130 | Fixtures in pytest? | Fixture, setup | • `@pytest.fixture def db(): yield conn; conn.close()`<br>• Tests receive fixture by parameter name<br>• Scopes: function, class, module, session<br>• `conftest.py` shares fixtures |\n",
"| 131 | `assert` statement? | Assert, debugging | • `assert expr, msg` → AssertionError if falsy<br>• Removed with `-O` flag<br>• For debugging invariants only<br>• **Never** for production validation |\n",
"| 132 | Module vs package vs library? | Module, library, framework | • **Module**: single .py file<br>• **Package**: directory + `__init__.py`<br>• **Library**: collection of packages (NumPy)<br>• **Framework**: controls flow (Django) |\n",
"| 133 | How does Python find modules? | sys.path, import | • Searches: current dir → PYTHONPATH → defaults<br>• `sys.path` lists search directories<br>• Can modify: `sys.path.append('/path')` |\n",
"| 134 | `__all__`? | __all__, public API | • Names exported by `from module import *`<br>• `__all__ = ['func1', 'Class1']`<br>• Hidden from `import *` but still importable directly |\n",
"| 135 | `abc` module? | ABC, abstract | • `from abc import ABC, abstractmethod`<br>• Abstract classes can't be instantiated<br>• Subclasses MUST implement `@abstractmethod`s<br>• Defines interfaces and contracts |\n",
"| 136 | `typing` module? | typing, generics | • `List[int]`, `Dict[str, int]`, `Optional[str]`<br>• `Callable[[int], str]`, `TypeVar('T')`<br>• `Protocol` for structural subtyping<br>• Python 3.9+: `list[int]`; 3.10+: `int \\| str` |\n",
"| 137 | `heapq`? | heapq, priority queue | • Min-heap: `heappush(h, val)`, `heappop(h)`<br>• Max-heap: negate values<br>• `nlargest(n, iter)`, `nsmallest(n, iter)`<br>• O(log n) push/pop<br>• Use for top-K, scheduling, Dijkstra |\n",
"| 138 | `bisect`? | bisect, binary search | • Binary search on sorted lists<br>• `bisect.bisect(a, x)` → insertion point<br>• `bisect.insort(a, x)` → insert maintaining sort<br>• O(log n) search |\n",
"| 139 | `hashlib`? | hashlib, SHA256 | • Cryptographic hashing: `hashlib.sha256(b'data').hexdigest()`<br>• SHA-256, SHA-512 (MD5 insecure)<br>• Passwords, file integrity, dedup<br>• Hashing is one-way, not encryption |\n",
"| 140 | Decorators with arguments? | Decorator, factory | • Three levels: `def repeat(n): def deco(fn): @wraps(fn) def wrap(*a): … return wrap return deco`<br>• Usage: `@repeat(3) def hello(): …`<br>• Outer function receives decorator args |\n",
"| 141 | `@lru_cache`? | lru_cache, memoize | • `@lru_cache(maxsize=128)` → cache results<br>• LRU evicts oldest when full<br>• `maxsize=None` → unlimited<br>• `.cache_info()` shows hits/misses<br>• Python 3.9+: `@cache` |\n",
"| 142 | `@contextmanager`? | contextlib, yield | • `@contextmanager def timer(): start=time(); yield; print(elapsed)`<br>• `yield` separates setup and cleanup<br>• Simpler than `__enter__/__exit__`<br>• `suppress(Error)` to ignore exceptions |\n",
"| 143 | `dataclasses.field`? | field, default_factory | • `field(default_factory=list)` → mutable defaults<br>• **Never** `field=[]` (shared reference!)<br>• `repr=False`, `compare=False`, `init=False`<br>• Fine-grained field control |\n",
"| 144 | `__slots__` when to use? | __slots__, memory | • `__slots__ = ['x', 'y']` replaces `__dict__`<br>• 20-30% less memory per instance<br>• Can't add dynamic attributes<br>• Use with millions of instances |\n",
"| 145 | `struct` module? | struct, binary | • Converts Python values ↔ C structs (bytes)<br>• `struct.pack('2i', 1, 2)` → bytes<br>• `struct.unpack('2i', data)` → tuple<br>• For binary files, network protocols |\n",
"| 146 | `inspect` module? | inspect, introspection | • `inspect.signature(func)` → parameters<br>• `inspect.getsource(func)` → source code<br>• `inspect.getmembers(obj)` → all attributes<br>• Used in debugging and frameworks |\n",
"| 147 | `__contains__` and `in`? | __contains__, in | • `__contains__` customizes `in` operator<br>• Without it: Python iterates via `__iter__`<br>• set/dict `in` is O(1); list is O(n) |\n",
"| 148 | `is None` vs `== None`? | is None, identity | • `is None` — identity check (exact None object)<br>• `== None` — calls `__eq__` (overridable)<br>• Always use `is None` (faster, unambiguous)<br>• PEP 8 recommends `is None` |\n",
"| 149 | `@property` in depth? | property, getter | • Getter: `@property def name(self):`<br>• Setter: `@name.setter def name(self, val):`<br>• Deleter: `@name.deleter def name(self):`<br>• Attribute-style access with method-level control |\n",
"| 150 | `__hash__` implementation? | __hash__, hashable | • Returns integer hash for dict keys / set elements<br>• If you define `__eq__`, MUST define `__hash__`<br>• `a == b` implies `hash(a) == hash(b)`<br>• Set to None for unhashable objects |\n",
