"---\n","\n",
"#### Section H: Design Patterns, Real-World & Data Science (Q176–200)\n",
"\n",
"| # | Question | Keywords | Answer |\n",
"|---|----------|----------|--------|\n",
"| 176 | Design patterns in Python? | Singleton, Factory | • **Creational**: Singleton, Factory, Builder<br>• **Structural**: Adapter, Decorator, Facade<br>• **Behavioral**: Observer, Strategy, Iterator<br>• Python's dynamism simplifies many patterns |\n",
"| 177 | Singleton pattern? | Singleton, single instance | • Ensures only one instance exists<br>• Python: use module (simplest), or `__new__` override<br>• Or `@lru_cache` on factory function<br>• Module-level is the Pythonic way |\n",
"| 178 | Factory pattern? | Factory, classmethod | • Creates objects without specifying exact class<br>• `@classmethod def from_json(cls, data): return cls(**data)`<br>• Decouples creation from usage |\n",
"| 179 | Observer pattern? | Observer, event | • Subjects notify observers on state changes<br>• Python: callbacks list, signals library<br>• UI components reacting to data changes |\n",
"| 180 | Iterator pattern? | Iterator, __iter__ | • Built-in Python support via `__iter__` + `__next__`<br>• Generators are simplest iterators<br>• `for` loop, `list()`, `sum()` all use iterator protocol |\n",
"| 181 | Strategy pattern? | Strategy, callable | • Swap algorithms at runtime<br>• In Python: just pass functions<br>• `def sort_data(data, strategy=sorted):`<br>• First-class functions make it trivial |\n",
"| 182 | Decorator pattern vs @? | Decorator, structural | • **Pattern**: wraps object to add behavior<br>• **@syntax**: wraps function<br>• `@decorator` IS the Decorator pattern for functions<br>• Both add behavior without modifying original |\n",
"| 183 | What is a coroutine? | Coroutine, async def | • Function that can be suspended and resumed<br>• `async def` defines, `await` pauses<br>• Cooperative (yield control explicitly)<br>• Single-threaded, non-blocking I/O |\n",
"| 184 | `asyncio.gather`? | gather, concurrent | • Runs multiple coroutines concurrently<br>• `results = await asyncio.gather(a(), b(), c())`<br>• Returns results in order<br>• `return_exceptions=True` catches errors |\n",
"| 185 | Event loop? | Event loop, asyncio | • Core of asyncio — runs coroutines<br>• `asyncio.run(main())` creates and runs loop<br>• One loop per thread<br>• Never block with CPU-heavy work<br>• Use `run_in_executor()` for blocking code |\n",
"| 186 | `concurrent.futures`? | ThreadPool, ProcessPool | • `ThreadPoolExecutor` → I/O-bound<br>• `ProcessPoolExecutor` → CPU-bound<br>• `executor.map(func, items)` → distribute work<br>• `submit()` returns `Future` objects |\n",
"| 187 | `multiprocessing.Pool`? | Pool, parallel | • `Pool(4)` creates process pool<br>• `pool.map(func, iter)` → true parallelism<br>• Each process has own GIL<br>• Data serialized (pickled) between processes |\n",
"| 188 | What is NumPy? | NumPy, ndarray | • N-dimensional arrays with vectorized ops<br>• 10-100x faster than lists for math<br>• Broadcasting, fancy indexing, linear algebra<br>• Core of scientific Python stack |\n",
"| 189 | What is Pandas? | Pandas, DataFrame | • `DataFrame` (2D table) and `Series` (1D)<br>• `read_csv()`, `groupby()`, `merge()`<br>• `pivot_table()`, `fillna()`, `apply()`<br>• Essential for data analysis |\n",
"| 190 | NumPy array vs list? | NumPy, list, perf | • NumPy: fixed type, contiguous, vectorized, fast<br>• List: mixed types, pointers, needs loops<br>• NumPy supports broadcasting<br>• 10-100x speed difference for math |\n",
"| 191 | Flask vs Django? | Flask, Django | • **Flask**: micro, minimal, flexible, good for APIs<br>• **Django**: full-featured, ORM, admin, auth, templates<br>• Flask for freedom; Django for rapid full-stack |\n",
"| 192 | REST API in Python? | REST, FastAPI | • HTTP methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE<br>• Frameworks: Flask, FastAPI, DRF<br>• FastAPI: async, auto-docs, type-validated<br>• Return JSON with status codes |\n",
"| 193 | `__getitem__`/`__setitem__`? | __getitem__, indexing | • `__getitem__` → `obj[key]`<br>• `__setitem__` → `obj[key] = val`<br>• `__delitem__` → `del obj[key]`<br>• Enables custom sequences/mappings |\n",
"| 194 | TensorFlow vs PyTorch? | TF, PyTorch, DL | • **TensorFlow**: Google, production-focused, TFLite<br>• **PyTorch**: Meta, research-friendly, dynamic graph<br>• PyTorch dominates research<br>• TensorFlow dominates production |\n",
"| 195 | List vs generator memory? | Generator, memory | • 1M ints: list ≈ 8MB, generator ≈ 120 bytes<br>• Gen computes on-the-fly (lazy)<br>• `sum(x**2 for x in range(10**6))` → O(1) memory<br>• Always prefer generators for large data |\n",
"| 196 | `timeit` module? | timeit, benchmark | • `timeit.timeit('code', number=1000)`<br>• More accurate than `time.time()`<br>• Disables GC, runs multiple iterations<br>• Jupyter: `%timeit expression` |\n",
"| 197 | `__enter__`/`__exit__`? | __enter__, __exit__ | • `__enter__` → setup, return resource<br>• `__exit__(exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb)` → cleanup<br>• Return True from `__exit__` to suppress exceptions<br>• `exc_type` is None if no exception |\n",
